class:: SchedulerBank
summary:: Coupled bank of sub-sample accurate event schedulers
related:: Classes/Phasor
categories:: UGens>Granular

description::
You can use SchedulerBank to generate multiple sub-sample accurate event streams which are coupled through a Kuramoto-Sakaguchi mean field.
SchedulerBank distributes the natural rates uniformly in log2 space around a base rate. 
A mean field is derived from the current scheduler phases and used to couple their rates in log2 space. 
The coupling strength is scaled relative to the spread of natural rates, while a phase lag can be used to bias the interaction.

SchedulerBank needs numChannels, a rate, spread, couple, bias and reset and outputs multichannel triggers, phases, rates and subSampleOffsets.
You can access the different outputs via their key from a dictionary.
SchedulerBank can be used for server side sub-sample accurate granulation.

classmethods::

method::ar

argument::numChannels
number of coupled schedulers (fixed with SynthDef evaluation)

argument::rate
base rate in Hz around which the natural scheduler rates are distributed

argument::spread
total spread of natural rates in log2 space.
A value of 1 distributes the rates across one octave, centered logarithmically around the base rate

argument::couple
coupling strength between the schedulers.
The coupling is scaled relative to the reference critical coupling derived from spread

argument::bias
Sakaguchi phase lag controlling the offset of the mean-field interaction.
A value of 0 gives no phase lag, while -1 and 1 correspond to -pi/2 and pi/2

argument::reset
trigger to reset all schedulers

returns:: triggers, phases, rates and subSampleOffsets.
The outputs can be accessed via key from a dictionary (e.g. events[\triggers], events[\phases], events[\rates], events[\subSampleOffsets])

SECTION::1) Examples - Plots

subsection::1.1) Distributed Ramps

code::
(
{
    var numSchedulers = 3;
    var events = SchedulerBank.ar(
        numChannels: numSchedulers,
        rate: \rate.kr(100),
        spread: \spread.kr(1),
        couple: \couple.kr(0),
        bias: \bias.kr(0)
    );
    events[\phases];
}.plot(0.041);
)
::

subsection::1.2) Coupled Ramps

code::
(
{
    var numSchedulers = 3;
    var events = SchedulerBank.ar(
        numChannels: numSchedulers,
        rate: \rate.kr(100),
        spread: \spread.kr(0.6),
        couple: \couple.kr(0.75),
        bias: \bias.kr(0)
    );
    events[\phases];
}.plot(0.041);
)
::

SECTION::2) Examples - Audio

subsection::2.1) Coupled Pulsars

code::
// load multi-cycle wavetable
(
t = Signal.sineFill(2048, [1], [0]);
u = Signal.sineFill(2048, 1.0/((1..512)**2)*([1,0,-1,0]!128).flatten);
w = Signal.sineFill(2048, 1.0/(1..512)*([1,0]!256).flatten);
x = Signal.sineFill(2048, 1.0/(1..512));
v = t.addAll(u).addAll(w).addAll(x);
b = Buffer.loadCollection(s, v);
)

(
{
    var numSchedulers = 3;
    var events, sig;

	events = SchedulerBank.ar(
		numChannels: numSchedulers,
		rate: \rate.kr(110),
		spread: \spread.kr(0.6),
		couple: MouseX.kr(0, 1),
		bias: MouseY.kr(-1, 1)
	);

	sig = DualPulsarOS.ar(

		trig: events[\triggers],
		triggerFreq: events[\rates] / \overlap.kr(1),
		subSampleOffset: events[\subSampleOffsets],

		oscFreq: \oscFreq.kr(440),
		modFreq: \modFreq.kr(440),

		pmIndexOsc: \pmIndexOsc.kr(0),
		pmIndexMod: \pmIndexMod.kr(0),
		pmFilterRatioOsc: 1,
		pmFilterRatioMod: 1,

		warpOsc: 0.5,
		warpMod: 0.5,

		oscBuffer: b,
		oscNumCycles: 4,
		oscCyclePos: 0,

		modBuffer: b,
		modNumCycles: 4,
		modCyclePos: 0,

		envSkew: 0.5,
		envIndex: 0,

		oversample: 1,
	);

	sig = sig.sum;

    sig = LeakDC.ar(sig);
    sig!2 * 0.1;
}.play;
)
::

subsection::2.2) Coupled and Overlapping Grains with Voice Allocation

code::
(
{
    var numSchedulers = 3;
    var numVoices = 5;
    var numSpeakers = 2;

    var reset, events, chains, sig;

    reset = Trig1.ar(\reset.tr(0), SampleDur.ir);

    events = SchedulerBank.ar(
        numChannels: numSchedulers,
        rate: \rate.kr(30),
        spread: \spread.kr(0.6),
        couple: \couple.kr(0.3),
        bias: \bias.kr(0),
        reset: reset
    );

    chains = numSchedulers.collect { |i|
        var voices;
        var grainFreqMod, grainFreqs, grainPhases, grainWindows;
        var grainOscs, grains, pan;

        voices = VoiceAllocator.ar(
            numChannels: numVoices,
            trig: events[\triggers][i],
            rate: events[\rates][i] / \overlap.kr(5),
            subSampleOffset: events[\subSampleOffsets][i],
        );

        grainWindows = HanningWindow.ar(
            phase: voices[\phases],
            skew: \skew.kr(0.03)
        );

        grainFreqMod = UnitStep.ar(voices[\phases]) * 2 - 1;
        grainFreqs = \freq.kr(800) * (2 ** (grainFreqMod * \freqMD.kr(2)));

        grainPhases = RampIntegrator.ar(
            trig: voices[\triggers],
            rate: grainFreqs,
            subSampleOffset: events[\subSampleOffsets][i]
        );

        grainOscs = SinOsc.ar(DC.ar(0), grainPhases * 2pi);
        grains = grainOscs * grainWindows;

        pan = UnitWalk.ar(voices[\phases]);
        grains = PanAz.ar(
            numChans: numSpeakers,
            in: grains,
            pos: pan.linlin(0, 1, -1 / numSpeakers, (2 * numSpeakers - 3) / numSpeakers)
        );
		
        grains.sum;
    };
	
	sig = (chains / numSchedulers).sum;

    sig = LeakDC.ar(sig);
    sig * 0.1;
}.play;
)
::
